{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA (rebar-debug Phase 1 — confirmed; analysis only, no fix applied)\n\nROOT CAUSE — show's read path never queries incoming edges. `show_state` (src/rebar/_engine_support/reads.py:277) = resolve → `reduce_ticket` → `public_state`, and `reduce_ticket` replays ONLY the subject ticket's own event directory. A LINK event is stored one-sided in the SOURCE ticket's dir (`process_link`, src/rebar/reducer/_processors.py:368 appends to state[\"deps\"] at :402), so for `A --blocks--> B` the edge exists solely in A's record: show B renders deps:[] with no trace of A. The data is NOT dropped downstream — it is never fetched. The read-only derivation that answers \"who points at me?\" already exists (`find_inbound_relationships`, src/rebar/reducer/_inbound.py:75), is exported in the public API (src/rebar/__init__.py:121) and consumed in production by the close gate (src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:136) — but no show/list/search path calls it. The NOTE at reads.py:290-293 documents this: the production dispatcher's show arm was never augmented with inbound_links; only a never-dispatched ticket-show.sh shim had the wiring, deleted in commit 28406790c6.\n\nDISCRIMINATING EVIDENCE (experiments run):\n1. Live store: `rebar show eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a` → deps show only OUTGOING (blocks→8625, relates_to→34cd); `rebar show 8625-7bea-67db-4cdc` (open, genuinely blocked) → deps:[] — looks unblocked; but `rebar deps 8625` → blockers:[dc0d…,eb58…,eb6e…], ready_to_work:false. Same CLI, contradictory impressions — reproduces the report exactly.\n2. Unit repro (fake store, A --blocks--> B): RAW reduce_ticket(B).deps=[] and no inbound key ever exists on the state (rejects H3 \"computed then stripped\" — unlike managed_refs, which IS stripped in _cmd_show, nothing inbound is ever computed); find_inbound_relationships(B) on the SAME store returns [{from_id:A, relation:blocks}] (rejects H2 \"data absent from store\"); show_state(B) contains no mention of A anywhere while find_ready_tickets correctly withholds B (confirms H1 \"never queried\"). ready's truth comes from a full-corpus edge inversion (`build_blocked_by`, src/rebar/graph/_relations.py:15) that show never consults.\n\nSIBLING SURFACES (named, not filed — all funnel through the same show_state): MCP `show_ticket` (src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py:48 → rebar.show_ticket → src/rebar/_reads.py:53 → show_state) SHARES the defect, as do the library facade `rebar.show_ticket`, the least-privilege agent tool (src/rebar/llm/pai_tools.py:219), `--output llm`, and list/search (same public_state shape). NOT affected: `rebar deps` / MCP `ticket_deps` (build_dep_graph returns blockers + ready_to_work) and `ready`/`next-batch`.\n\nLEGITIMACY — defect-class (incomplete-implementation). Inbound-in-show was designed and built: the RED test suite is titled \"inbound-relationship derivation (ticket show completeness)\" (tests/scripts/reducer/test_ticket_inbound.py:1), the llm formatter still carries the inbound_links→ibl mapping (src/rebar/reducer/llm_format.py:50), and the shim contained the exact wiring (state['inbound_links']=…) but was never dispatched — built-but-unwired. Honest counter-citation: commit 28406790c6 dropped that wiring marked \"intended, breaking window\", and test_reducer_single_source pins the show==list==search shape — but that intent is refactor hygiene (collapse dual read paths, preserve production shape), not a product rationale that blocked-ness should be invisible; no such rationale exists anywhere. The gap causes documented operational failure (this ticket: doomed dispatch of a subagent onto a blocked ticket).\n\nPHASE-2 SKETCH: derive at read time in show_state (no stored mirror, so nothing can drift): compute blocked_by via the SAME build_blocked_by inversion ready uses (single source ⇒ show and ready cannot disagree — AC#5), plus a direction-labeled inbound_links list from find_inbound_relationships (generalises to discovered_from/duplicates; relates_to dedupe already handled by _SYMMETRIC_RELATIONS in _inbound.py). Cost note: find_inbound_relationships byte-scans the corpus — fine per-show, too hot for list N tickets ⇒ scope augmentation to show and deliberately amend the test_reducer_single_source contract (show ⊇ list shape), documenting the decision per AC#4. Extend TicketStateOut so MCP show_ticket surfaces it; llm format reuses the existing ibl mapping.\n\nSUGGESTED RED ORACLE: tmp tracker with A --blocks--> B; assert (1) show_state(B)/`rebar show B --output json` exposes blocker A with unambiguous direction (blocked_by contains A; deps unchanged), (2) same fixture pins show against find_ready_tickets: B absent from ready ⇔ show(B) reports it blocked. Fails today — proven: show_state(B) deps:[] and A appears nowhere in its output."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785797857456670001,"uuid":"65f4547c-cd49-431d-a997-43fc9e436d00"}